A||||10||88formula||GM vs aftermarket slave cylinders. Please help||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||08-08-2001||09:34 PM||||I have a problem with my clutch not disengaging completely. I have been through countless threads in the search database. I did read a lot of threads that claim that when someone switched an aftermarket slave cylinder with a GM one there clutch worked normally.<P>I have an aftermarket slave cylinder. I have modified my clutch pedal to give the master cylinder more travel. I have bleed this thing countless times in every possible way. I have a new master cylinder. When I measure the travel at the slave cylinder right on top of the clutch fork arm I get 13/16-inch travel; I should have 7/8-1-1/8 inch travel. I can't think of anything else that could give me more travel without modifying the clutch hydraulic system.<P>So I would really, really, really appreciate it if as many of you forum members could tell me if switching your aftermarket slave cylinder for a GM slave solved your problem. I can't afford to just keep throwing parts at this thing only to find out nothing changed.<P>If I don't fix this clutch soon I'm going to rip it out, blast it with a shotgun and put in an automatic transmission.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||PJ||08-08-2001||09:55 PM||||I feel your pain, believe me. Unfortunately all I can tell you is that whenever I've replaced the '88 slave I've used the GM/Delco unit without any problems. I have tried aftermarket master cylinders though that were useless. The mechanic at the dealership who I've known for over 15 years says the CAST aftermarket cylinders aren't worth crap. He only uses the MACHINED OEM ones. After being burned more than once I now strongly agree with him.<P>PJ<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Archie||08-08-2001||10:35 PM||||#1 "If it ain't Delco, I't won't go!"<P>#2 Read the 3 clutch threads on this page <A HREF="http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A> <P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||08-09-2001||08:09 AM||||I replaced my slave cylinder for my 4spd with a NAPA replacement.  The replacement fit right into place and the clutch worked exactly like normal without any modifications to anything.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||08-09-2001||10:52 AM||||i just replaced master and slave cyls. with gm parts. i had brough some before from i think pepboys and auto zone about 1 1/2 years ago and had lost clutch action recently. seems to have done the trick.<P>for got to add that i tried two or three ways of bleeding it and ended up jacking up the front and letting it gravity bleed for a while. made a big difference.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 08-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||starfighter007@msn.com||08-09-2001||12:00 PM||||I have not had the pleasure of having to deal with a clutch hyd. problem on my car yet, Knock on wood. Archie is ussually quiet around here when he does speak I listen to what he has to say.I met him last oct.on a trip to chicago,he is very knowledgeable about fieros. and if I remember correctly He has a engineering back ground In the aviation industry.So If he says its gonna snow in july here in Phx. I am gettin me a snow shovel,and delco clutch components||||reg||10
Z||000006||LivoniaFieroGT||08-09-2001||12:32 PM||||Had similar problem and the root cause was a worn t/o beraing.  One side of the bearing was worn and bent and the fork would not complete the necessary travel.   If I pumped the pedal it was sometimes enough.  Check the T/O bearing as the cause.  Of course you need to drop the cradle and pull the trans.... minor weekend assignment.||||reg||1
Z||000007||LivoniaFieroGT||08-09-2001||12:33 PM||||Had similar problem and the root cause was a worn t/o beraing.  One side of the bearing was worn and bent and the fork would not complete the necessary travel.   If I pumped the pedal it was sometimes enough.  Check the T/O bearing as the cause.  Of course you need to drop the cradle and pull the trans.... minor weekend assignment.  Also maybe the clutch fork is bent or worn<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||08-09-2001||04:59 PM||||Thanks for your response guys. My clutch, throwout bearing, and pressure plate are all new. <P>Archie, thank you for the link to your archism. The part about pushing the slave pushrod in I didn't do. I'll try that next.<BR> <BR>Archie do you know how much travel you usually get on fieros equipped with five speed Getrag transmissions at the slave cylinder. In your archism it states that the slave cylinder needs to travel 1.15 inches to work properly? Is that distance for the Getrag transmission?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||KissMySSFiero||08-09-2001||06:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PJ:<BR><B>. The mechanic at the dealership who I've known for over 15 years says the CAST aftermarket cylinders aren't worth crap. He only uses the MACHINED OEM ones. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>so where can you get a machined one.  im looking for one soon.  are there any out there that are oem quality without going to a dealer.  anyone have a link to get a delco part.  im not gonna go with the discount lifetime one at all.  ive had my car for about 4 yrs and ive put on 3 of these.  one was a direct gm part but my dumbass twisted the bleeder off.  <P>has anyone tried the autozone or napa parts.<BR>autozone is $68(probably worth every penny)  napa is $145 and is new.  havent checked a dealer yet.<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000010||DRH||08-09-2001||06:49 PM||||I put a NAPA one on a couple of years ago. After I <B>finally</B> got it bled, it worked fine for 6 months or so then started not engaging right. I assumed it was air in the line and started bleeding again. I broke the bleeder boss off overtightening it (oops, shouldn't tighten stuff while pi$$ed    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">). This is where the lifetime warranty came in handy though. They replaced the one I broke, no questions asked!<P>BTW, it turned out the <B>real</B> problem was that one of the bolts holding the mounting bracket on had come loose and was letting it push back just enough to not work right. You might want to check that.<P>On the second cylinder, after trying gravity bleed and several other methods, I still couldn't get a good pedal. I think leaking air around the threads of the bleeder when you loosen it is part of the problem. My wife (aka clutch pedal pusher) finally gave up and went to bed. I wound up using my little bleeder cup hooked to manifold vacuum to bleed it. Worked like a charm! No problems since.<P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 08-09-2001).]||||reg||1
